WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.166 --> 00:00:09.167
I want you to think
about the image that you see

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.083
when I say one word:

00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:12.375
migrant.

00:00:14.292 --> 00:00:16.833
You may have pictured
a crowded boat in rough waters,

00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:19.000
people clinging
to the top of a freight train

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.792
or crossing a desert
wearing worn-out shoes.

00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:24.042
This is what we see in the news cycle,

00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:27.667
24 hours, day after day,
story after story.

00:00:27.708 --> 00:00:32.625
People who are desperate, fleeing wars,
fleeing climate change, fleeing poverty.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:36.458
But in reality, most people
move for more common reasons:

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:42.750
to get a good education, to find a job,
to find family members or to fall in love.

00:00:42.791 --> 00:00:44.250
And this is nothing new.

00:00:44.291 --> 00:00:48.125
Archaeologists like me
have been studying migration

00:00:48.166 --> 00:00:51.166
and finding that people for hundreds
and even thousands of years

00:00:51.208 --> 00:00:52.875
have been moving around the globe.

00:00:52.916 --> 00:00:56.500
From Europe's earliest farmers
to Vikings to pirates,

00:00:56.541 --> 00:00:59.583
Roman gladiators
and even Neanderthal cavemen,

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.750
and people like you and me.

00:01:01.791 --> 00:01:04.833
Mobility is one of the things
that makes us human.

00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:06.750
People move.

00:01:06.791 --> 00:01:10.417
And we know this because of something
that you brought with you here tonight.

00:01:10.458 --> 00:01:13.583
You carry it with you to many places:
to work, to the gym, to bed,

00:01:13.583 --> 00:01:15.208
and even in the shower.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.708
It’s not your cell phone,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.792
and it's not in your purse
or your pockets.

00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:22.625
It's you. It's your body
and your bones, all 206 of them.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:26.125
I brought mine and in case for some reason
you didn’t bring yours along,

00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:27.583
we do have an extra one here

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:30.375
because your bones will tell
the story of your life,

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:32.500
even a single tooth.

00:01:32.542 --> 00:01:34.792
And we know
that teeth tell us many things.

00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:36.458
For your dentist, for example.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.333
He or she can see if you floss

00:01:38.375 --> 00:01:42.125
or if, like me, you really like candy,
and you might end up with some cavities.

00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:45.042
And if my dentist is here tonight,
yes, I will see you Monday.

00:01:45.042 --> 00:01:46.292
And I've been very good.

00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:49.167
But your teeth also tell you
something about migration.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:52.500
If you take your tongue
and run it along your incisors,

00:01:52.542 --> 00:01:53.792
these front teeth,

00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:58.333
the back of those will be flat
if you have European or African ancestry,

00:01:58.375 --> 00:02:01.042
if you feel a sort of scoop
or shovel shape,

00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:04.500
your ancestors may have been
Native American or migrated from Asia.

00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:07.042
If we go inside the tooth
to the pulp cavity,

00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:08.917
we may be able to extract the DNA

00:02:08.958 --> 00:02:12.542
and see if your ancestors came
from Egypt or England or both.

00:02:13.375 --> 00:02:14.958
But we're not interested as much

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.042
in your family's
migration history as yours.

00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:20.125
And that's where we
go to the tooth enamel,

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:21.417
what it's made out of,

00:02:21.458 --> 00:02:23.417
to try and find out if a person moved

00:02:23.458 --> 00:02:25.458
and even when they moved.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.958
And it's based on one simple idea:
that you are what you eat.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.208
All the minerals
and the elements in the food,

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.167
like calcium, oxygen,
which is the O in H2O,

00:02:34.208 --> 00:02:35.458
sodium and salt,

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.333
can tell us something about your diet.

00:02:37.375 --> 00:02:39.625
So we know if you like
cornbread or white bread,

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:43.583
if you prefer pork, chicken
or if you really like seafood.

00:02:43.625 --> 00:02:46.625
There are other elements
that tell us where that food came from,

00:02:46.625 --> 00:02:49.375
and that includes sulfur,
strontium, oxygen and even lead,

00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:51.583
which, of course,
you don't want very much of.

00:02:51.583 --> 00:02:53.625
But these tell us
where the food comes from

00:02:53.667 --> 00:02:56.500
and that can tell us where you were
when you were eating it.

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:59.708
And that is what archaeologists use
to identify ancient migration.

00:03:00.708 --> 00:03:02.708
If we look inside the tooth enamel,

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.667
we can see, for example,
in your first molar --

00:03:05.708 --> 00:03:08.542
this is the one that was forming
along with your baby teeth,

00:03:08.583 --> 00:03:10.500
but it's the only one you still have --

00:03:10.542 --> 00:03:13.042
and that tells us
where you were living as an infant.

00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:16.125
If we look at the wisdom tooth,
which is the last tooth to form,

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:19.958
that enamel would have been mineralizing
just before you hit your teenage years.

00:03:19.958 --> 00:03:21.958
So we know where you're living then.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.083
If we look at your bones --

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:27.875
and in that pause,
you just formed some new bone cells --

00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:30.625
that’s telling us what you’re eating
and what you’re doing

00:03:30.625 --> 00:03:32.458
in just about the past decade of life,

00:03:32.458 --> 00:03:34.917
so we can really track where people moved.

00:03:34.958 --> 00:03:38.458
And we've looked at this
for hundreds and thousands of individuals

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.250
to identify migration in the past.

00:03:40.292 --> 00:03:43.208
So I'd like to introduce you
to some ancient migrants.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.833
If we go back in time 1600 years,
we can go to the city of Copán,

00:03:46.875 --> 00:03:49.583
where the Maya people lived
in what's now Honduras.

00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:51.958
If we came around the year 400 AD,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.333
we might have walked into broad plazas
under a really hot tropical sun

00:03:55.375 --> 00:03:57.833
that was shining
onto bright red-painted buildings

00:03:57.875 --> 00:04:00.500
that had altars and carved statues
in front of them.

00:04:00.542 --> 00:04:02.208
If we'd come on the right day,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.958
we might have seen the inauguration
of ruler K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo',

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.208
roughly translated, that means
Sun-Faced First Macaw.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.167
The Maya rulers had really great names:

00:04:11.208 --> 00:04:14.708
Bird Jaguar, Dark Sun, Lady Great Skull.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.542
But what was really neat
about Yax K'uk' Mo'

00:04:17.583 --> 00:04:21.917
is that he established a dynasty
that lasted for more than 400 years.

00:04:21.958 --> 00:04:25.167
And every depiction
we have of him, as you see here,

00:04:25.208 --> 00:04:26.875
shows him in foreign clothing.

00:04:26.917 --> 00:04:29.125
This is what people wore
in central Mexico,

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.917
which was not in the Maya region
and actually hundreds of miles away.

00:04:32.958 --> 00:04:37.167
So for a long time, archaeologists thought
that this was a foreign king.

00:04:37.208 --> 00:04:39.042
But his teeth told a different story.

00:04:39.083 --> 00:04:42.208
By sampling his first molar,
his wisdom tooth and bone,

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.792
we found that he, in fact, probably came
from somewhere in the Maya region.

00:04:45.833 --> 00:04:48.875
So he was a migrant,
but he may have lived in multiple places

00:04:48.917 --> 00:04:50.625
before coming to live at Copán,

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:54.083
even though he dressed
like he was a foreigner,

00:04:54.125 --> 00:04:55.792
someone from Mexico.

00:04:55.833 --> 00:04:59.208
And I always think about when
the Chicago Cubs were in the World Series

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.250
and people went out and bought Cubs gear,

00:05:01.292 --> 00:05:03.542
even though maybe
they'd never been to Chicago.

00:05:03.583 --> 00:05:04.875
It's a good look.

00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:06.875
But let me take you to another Maya city.

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:09.042
And this is where I work,
called Xunantunich.

00:05:09.083 --> 00:05:12.125
In 2016, archaeologists found
the largest tomb ever discovered

00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:15.125
in the country, in Belize --
that's where Xunantunich is.

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:18.542
And inside that tomb,
there were the remains of a ruler,

00:05:18.583 --> 00:05:19.833
but also jade,

00:05:19.875 --> 00:05:23.375
the kind of plates and the kind of vessels
that you might see in a museum,

00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:26.333
and jaguar bones, which symbolize
possibly royal power.

00:05:26.375 --> 00:05:30.417
This person may have actually
even been wearing a jaguar pelt cape.

00:05:30.458 --> 00:05:32.583
But Xunantunich was a fairly small city.

00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:36.417
So there was a possibility
that this was a foreign ruler.

00:05:36.458 --> 00:05:39.917
But in fact, looking at the DNA
and looking at the tooth enamel,

00:05:39.958 --> 00:05:41.500
this was no foreign king.

00:05:41.542 --> 00:05:43.833
She was a Maya queen

00:05:43.875 --> 00:05:46.917
or some other royalty
who was probably local.

00:05:46.958 --> 00:05:50.750
We actually don't usually find
foreign kings and queens.

00:05:50.792 --> 00:05:52.875
But I told you the story
was about migrants.

00:05:52.917 --> 00:05:54.292
And it is, because, in fact,

00:05:54.333 --> 00:05:55.958
it was the commoners who moved.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.667
Up to 25 percent of the population
of every village and every city

00:05:59.708 --> 00:06:02.375
consisted of migrants --
men, women and children

00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:04.833
who'd moved, sometimes
from multiple places,

00:06:04.875 --> 00:06:06.792
to live in the same household.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:08.917
Migration seemed
to be common among the Maya

00:06:08.958 --> 00:06:11.458
and many ancient civilizations.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.208
But we can move forward in time
to the year 1493,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.833
when Christopher Columbus
set off on his second voyage

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:18.667
to establish a trading outpost.

00:06:18.708 --> 00:06:24.833
He left with 17 ships and 1,200 men,
nobility, clergy, sailors and craftsmen

00:06:24.875 --> 00:06:27.417
to establish what
we now know as La Isabela,

00:06:27.458 --> 00:06:29.000
named after Queen Isabella,

00:06:29.042 --> 00:06:31.292
in the northern part
of the Dominican Republic,

00:06:31.333 --> 00:06:34.125
or La Española, as they called it then.

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:37.917
Things were not good:
food was scarce, disease was rampant

00:06:37.958 --> 00:06:40.833
and Columbus may not have been
the best manager,

00:06:40.875 --> 00:06:42.417
so mortality rates were high.

00:06:42.458 --> 00:06:43.917
And by the 1980s and 90s,

00:06:43.958 --> 00:06:46.667
when archaeologists began
to excavate the settlement

00:06:46.708 --> 00:06:47.958
and then the cemetery,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.292
they started to wonder:

00:06:49.333 --> 00:06:53.125
Did the ships’ rosters include
everyone who is buried here?

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:55.125
Who actually lived at La Isabela?

00:06:56.125 --> 00:06:58.250
My job came a bit later
as a graduate student

00:06:58.292 --> 00:07:00.500
when I was tasked
with going around the island

00:07:00.542 --> 00:07:03.875
to try and collect samples that could
serve as proxies for human teeth.

00:07:03.917 --> 00:07:05.417
What would the enamel look like

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:07.542
of the people who were
living at La Isabela?

00:07:07.583 --> 00:07:09.375
Someone else got to go to Spain,

00:07:09.417 --> 00:07:11.292
but I rented a car, drove around,

00:07:11.333 --> 00:07:14.083
went into the mountains,
drove toward the river valleys.

00:07:14.125 --> 00:07:16.042
And of course, I went to a beach or two.

00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:17.833
And everything was going really well

00:07:17.875 --> 00:07:21.042
until the last day when I came
to what was supposed to be a bridge

00:07:21.083 --> 00:07:22.792
and saw a river and a herd of cows.

00:07:22.792 --> 00:07:27.375
So it was getting dark.
I was by myself. I was low on gas.

00:07:27.417 --> 00:07:30.250
And after I saw some cowboys
on the other side of the river

00:07:30.292 --> 00:07:31.625
kind of waving their hands,

00:07:31.667 --> 00:07:33.750
I just rolled down the window
and gunned it.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.542
And I made it across
and I had the samples,

00:07:36.583 --> 00:07:39.292
which was a good thing
because there were some surprises.

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:42.542
We did find soldiers
and people from Spain, probably Andalusia,

00:07:42.583 --> 00:07:44.042
where Columbus sailed from,

00:07:44.083 --> 00:07:46.125
and maybe other parts
of the Mediterranean.

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:48.708
But we also found
an indigenous Taíno woman,

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.000
a local one,

00:07:50.042 --> 00:07:52.417
and other women who came from Europe,

00:07:52.458 --> 00:07:53.708
and one person who,

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:57.292
if we can get some DNA
to see if this is actually true,

00:07:57.333 --> 00:07:59.042
may have been from Africa.

00:07:59.083 --> 00:08:01.875
None of these people
were on the ships' rosters.

00:08:01.917 --> 00:08:05.042
Teeth can tell us things
that the history books leave out.

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:07.625
Our final story brings us
back closer to Mississippi

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:10.208
to an abandoned cemetery
just west of Jackson,

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.625
where we can learn
about the lives of the settlers

00:08:12.667 --> 00:08:14.333
who lived here in the early 1800s.

00:08:14.375 --> 00:08:16.083
Now, some of them came with wealth.

00:08:16.125 --> 00:08:20.375
They could erect tombstones and write
their life histories on their graves.

00:08:20.417 --> 00:08:22.750
If they were married,
whether they had children,

00:08:22.792 --> 00:08:25.333
where they came from
and sometimes even how they died,

00:08:25.375 --> 00:08:28.708
as cholera and yellow fever epidemics
swept through the region.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.792
Richard lived until age 56.
He died in 1849.

00:08:33.667 --> 00:08:35.458
Charles had a shorter life.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:38.916
In 1855, he'd survived only to age 29.

00:08:39.666 --> 00:08:41.041
But these men were planters,

00:08:41.083 --> 00:08:43.833
and we know that they brought
enslaved people with them.

00:08:43.875 --> 00:08:45.125
Where were they buried?

00:08:45.166 --> 00:08:47.708
And what about the poor farmers
and the sharecroppers?

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.958
There were more than 350 graves
in the cemetery.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.250
And so we decided to do our best
to solve these historic forensic cases

00:08:55.291 --> 00:08:57.916
and reconstruct the lives
of the people who lived there.

00:08:57.958 --> 00:09:00.250
And one of these people,
one of the migrants,

00:09:00.291 --> 00:09:02.458
was a man buried in grave 3.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.916
He was of African descent.

00:09:04.958 --> 00:09:07.125
He lived into the middle,
maybe even old age,

00:09:07.166 --> 00:09:09.583
by the time he died in the early 1900s.

00:09:10.083 --> 00:09:11.875
He was probably born into slavery.

00:09:12.625 --> 00:09:14.958
And so we wonder,
was he brought to Mississippi?

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.458
Was he taken from his family
like so many enslaved children were?

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:22.041
Or did he come after the Civil War
to make a new life for himself,

00:09:22.083 --> 00:09:23.500
or find family?

00:09:23.541 --> 00:09:25.875
He and the men
with similar life histories,

00:09:25.916 --> 00:09:30.208
migrants who are buried
in grave 18 and grave 219,

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:31.750
they made a good enough living

00:09:31.791 --> 00:09:34.416
that their families were able
to give them nice burials,

00:09:34.458 --> 00:09:36.166
maybe even expensive ones.

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:37.958
So even if we can't say their names,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.416
we can at least tell something
of their stories.

00:09:41.083 --> 00:09:43.250
And migration is part of my story, too.

00:09:43.291 --> 00:09:44.916
My grandparents came from Germany.

00:09:44.958 --> 00:09:48.125
My grandpa in 1923, to join his uncle.

00:09:48.166 --> 00:09:49.791
And my grandma a few years later.

00:09:49.833 --> 00:09:51.625
We just found their travel documents

00:09:51.666 --> 00:09:53.833
and a picture of my grandma
on a lounge chair

00:09:53.875 --> 00:09:57.333
on one of the big ships
that sailed across the Atlantic.

00:09:57.375 --> 00:09:59.916
We actually don't know
exactly why they came,

00:09:59.958 --> 00:10:01.833
but I imagine it's for the same reason

00:10:01.875 --> 00:10:04.250
that people come today
to the United States:

00:10:04.291 --> 00:10:06.250
because they had a hunger and a hope,

00:10:06.291 --> 00:10:09.791
if not to make life better for themselves,
to do it for their kids.

00:10:10.708 --> 00:10:15.541
They met in Chicago, got married
and had two boys, one of those was my dad.

00:10:15.583 --> 00:10:19.958
I’m the third generation of my family
to be here, and I’m also a migrant.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.375
I've come less than 10 years ago
from the Midwest to live in Mississippi.

00:10:24.416 --> 00:10:28.916
So whether your family has
an ancient migration story

00:10:28.958 --> 00:10:31.291
or whether you look
in the mirror and see yours,

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:34.083
remember that every time
you smile and show your teeth,

00:10:34.125 --> 00:10:35.375
you're sharing that.

00:10:35.416 --> 00:10:36.666
Thank you.

